      "projects": [
        {
          "title": "Year 4 Individual Project - Incremental Slow Feature Analysis for Visual Localisation",
          "subtitle": "Oct 2015 - Jun 2016",
          "subComponenets": [
            {
              "description": [
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Associated with Imperial College London"
                },
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Explored the use of incremental slow feature analysis as an on-line unsupervised processor for real-time vision-based machine localisation and navigation.\n\nThis project was conducted under the supervision of Dr Anil Bharath in the Biologically Inspired Computer Vision research group at Imperial College London. "
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Year 3 Group Project - Static Camera Based Indoor Positioning System",
          "subtitle": "Oct 2014 - Jun 2015",
          "subComponenets": [
            {
              "description": [
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Associated with Imperial College London"
                },
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "The blind and partially sighted (BPS) community faces many challenges in navigating through daily life that could be alleviated through assisted-living technologies, increasing independence. As it is desirable to have an IPS that uses ubiquitous technologies such as static cameras and wearable sensors, our project aimed to explore challenges in implementing such a system and prototype a proof-of-concept. We designed a system where an accelerometry signal from a user’s mobile device was compared to accelerometry signals derived from the visual tracking of individuals in order to identify the user. \n\nThis project was conducted under the supervision of Dr Anil Bharath in the Biologically Inspired Computer Vision research group at Imperial College London. "
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Year 2 Engineering Design Project - Horse Riding Simulator",
          "subtitle": "Oct 2013 - Jun 2014",
          "subComponenets": [
            {
              "description": [
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "Associated with Imperial College London"
                },
                {
                  "type": "textComponent",
                  "text": "The objective of our project was to design and implement a horse riding simulator which could be used as both an introductory tool for novice riders and as a device for equine-assisted therapy. \n\nOur simulator was designed to approximate the elliptical motion of a real horse and this was achieved mechanically through the use of a crankshaft that controls horizontal and vertical displacements of the \"horse\" body. We verified the simulator’s function through video motion analysis and compared it to the data from a real horse found in the literature. "
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
